<b>Interview 17: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;：magazine editor, female, born in 1970s, Beijing</b> 

<p><b>What do you think of the Kassel Exhibition?</b>
<br>This Documenta is an unsafe exhibition and it is full of adventure and subversion. It is a rebellion which embodies text tradition and is initiated by European artists to fight against U.S. MOMA style exhibitions famous for commercialization, visualization, standardization and stimulation. In addition, it united 50% Asian, African, South African and East European artists as well as a history dated back to 3000 years before. This exhibition is similar to a visual art version of Habermas's view. The display and selection of works present a pure European taste. Such a worldwide large scale exhibition is not just a question of art, but it is more of a presentation of cultural and political strategy. What makes Fairytale interesting is that it utilized a variety of complex and unequal relations among countries and populations.

<p><b>How do you see faith and our cultural tradition?</b>
<br>I learned calligraphy and Chinese painting from an old scholar since I was young and I especially adored Lao-tzu and Zhuangzi. Later then, when the spirit of Taoist could not comfort my pain anymore, I began to read works on Buddhism. But it is not faith. I do not really need a stronger and more complete outside ideology to influence myself. I still believe the instant judgment made by body or intuition and look forward to the coming of new possibilities. Maybe it is because I have never obtained a deep and profound religious experience which is said can exceed love.
<p>Tradition is preserved in books and it is definite that it cannot be delivered anymore. We undoubtedly have lost the essence of Chinese traditional culture and kept the worst part. In real life of Modern China, tradition is disappearing in a comprehensive life system composed of by architecture, music, clothing, entertainment and behavior. What called it back artificially are just some concepts. I do hope the essence still exists in someone or some special area and it can be carried forward sometime in future.
<p>China and the Western World may not have same core values. This difference may begin from Greek Mythology, Homer and the Book of Changes. China in particular has not been nurtured by the values of the Enlightenment. The present core issue of China might be how to face post-colonial and paradoxical problems that appear in global modernization, such as environment, population, resources, institution and economy.

<p><b>How do you perceive education?</b>
<br>Education is another problem arousing the West's critical concern. Chinese education has never been satisfactory. Education has little impact on me and I kept discarding it after I learned it. Compared with college education, I intend to believe that self-education can truly influence people.

<p><b>Are you married? Do you still have illusions?</b>
<br>I am married so I have no fantasy for love. But I have fantasy for arts though it also has falseness and bad content. We have so much dissatisfaction with art that this fantasy might be unnecessary. 

<p><b>Have you ever had this doubt? What kind of doubt?</b>
<br>Because I work on magazines, I have opportunities to meet different people involved in art, such as artists, gallery owners, auctioneers and professors. The life of these people is somewhat related to art, however, they have their respective values which are totally different from each other. It seems that everyone lives in his or her own world and it is a fully self-contained system. They move forward according to their own system and live confidently as it should be like this. However, when you penetrate deeply into these kinds of values systems, you will discover the unreliability and variety of these values systems. When I leave my own life which I take it for granted and enter into other's values system, the sharp contrast has great impact on me. Sometimes I even become suspicious of my own values. For instance, a gallery owner is a business man which will be happy with making a fortune while a professor may set doing outstanding research as his goal. It looks unreal when you compare the two and sometimes I even feel unreal about art itself. From time to time, I feel that I may become a complete nihilist and it worried me for a while.
<p>Contemporary Chinese art has not obtained market in the past. Recently, "artwork market" has become very hot and some artists who once received ironic criticism because of their insistence on idealism become stars of the market within two or three years. More speculators joined as well as people who truly have faith in art. These people are mixed together with a mingling of good and evil, which opened the possibility of Chinese contemporary art to the largest extent.

<p><b>Do you like your present job?</b>
<br>I like my present job. I hope I could make the best art magazine in China while remaining in a neutral position. I may broadcast some effective information mastered by a few people about creating art to all people in favorite of art and working for art.

<p><b>How do you perceive wealth？</b>
<br>Wealth is not that important to me. It is possible that I am not accustomed to the business and consumer age and I simply would like to lead a life which could meet basic needs and is relatively comfortable and convenient. Making money for me is not much different from obtaining small praise from a primary school teacher, both of which are rewarding games within the institution.

